<LearningGuideLayout title="How to Build Godot Levels in Blender" description="A dependable Blender-to-Godot level workflow covering scale, transforms, naming, collision, glTF exports, sub-scenes, and gameplay metadata." summary="Blender is excellent for spatial composition; Godot is excellent for runtime behavior. A reliable pipeline gives each tool a clear job and makes re-exporting safe enough to do throughout development." relatedSlugs={["blender-to-godot-gameplay-import","godot-scene-metadata","instant-game-mechanics"]} godotSource={{label:"Model export considerations",href:"https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_3d_scenes/model_export_considerations.html"}}>
    <div class="guide-summary"><strong>The goal is repeatable re-export.</strong> If updating a wall means rebuilding triggers and scripts in Godot, the level has two competing sources of truth. Keep authored geometry and placement in Blender, and keep unique runtime systems in stable Godot scenes or scripts.</div>
    <h2>Establish the coordinate contract</h2><p>Godot uses Y up and forward along negative Z for cameras; glTF defines consistent asset orientation between tools. Work at real scale, apply transforms where appropriate, and verify one known-size object before building a whole map. For oriented assets, test forward direction early rather than repairing every character later.</p>
    <h2>A practical level workflow</h2><ol><li>Block the walkable spaces with simple geometry and test scale in Godot immediately.</li><li>Choose stable, readable names for rooms, entrances, spawn points, and interactive props.</li><li>Separate render meshes from simple collision intent; avoid using detailed concave geometry for every object.</li><li>Apply or deliberately preserve transforms before export. Be consistent across reusable assets.</li><li>Export GLB for a compact single-file handoff, with custom properties enabled when gameplay metadata is required.</li><li>Split large interiors or regions into sub-scenes with explicit load and unload boundaries.</li><li>Re-export often. A pipeline only becomes trustworthy by surviving ordinary iteration.</li></ol>
    <h2>Decide what belongs where</h2><ul><li><strong>Blender:</strong> geometry, transforms, materials, cameras, lights, animation, placement, and authored gameplay annotations.</li><li><strong>Godot:</strong> game-wide managers, UI, save state, unique scripts, platform integration, and runtime-only effects.</li><li><strong>Shared contract:</strong> stable IDs, event names, item kinds, scene paths, and feature types.</li></ul>
    <h2>Common mistakes</h2><ul><li>Making manual edits inside an imported scene that disappear on reimport.</li><li>Using one giant level file when rooms have natural streaming or ownership boundaries.</li><li>Exporting unapplied negative scales or unpredictable triangulation.</li><li>Letting filenames and cross-scene IDs drift independently.</li></ul>
    <Fragment slot="fpe"><p>FPE makes Blender the authoring surface for common level mechanics as well as placement. Add triggers, speakers, players, inventory items, holdable props, scene settings, sub-scenes, and frame events through Blender panels. Export standard GLB and load it beneath one <code>FoldedPaperEngine</code> node. Whole-level and sub-scene commands keep re-exportable content separate from your stable Godot shell.</p><p><a href="/docs/fpe-subscenes-and-levels.html">Read the sub-scenes and level loading guide</a>.</p></Fragment>
</LearningGuideLayout>
